.\" Podėlis manual page. Keep the options in step with cli.rs's USAGE (a
.\" test checks that every option there is listed here).
.\" View with: man ./docs/podelis.1
.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt PODELIS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm podelis
.Nd terminal SQL client for PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Op Ar connection
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Op Ar connection
.Fl e Ar sql
.Op Fl f Ar format
.Op Fl -limit Ar n
.Op Fl y
.Op Fl -create
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Op Ar connection
.Fl -import Ar file
.Op Fl -table Ar name
.Op Fl y
.Op Fl -create
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Op Ar connection
.Fl -test
.Nm
.Cm doctor
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Op Fl -connections
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Cm ask
.Op Fl -model Ar name
.Ar connection question
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Cm memory
.Op Cm list Oo Ar words Oc | Cm add Ar subject what | Cm forget Ar id | Cm confirm Ar id | Cm learn Ar folder | Cm export
.Nm
.Cm gateway
.Op Cm status | start | stop | models
.Nm
.Cm gateway key Ar model
.Nm
.Cm gateway test Op Ar model
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar project
.Cm gateway context Ar connection question
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Without
.Fl e ,
.Fl -import ,
.Fl -test
or
.Cm doctor ,
.Nm
starts its terminal user interface: projects of saved connections, a SQL
editor with completion and lint warnings, and a result grid.
Press
.Ic F1
for help and
.Ic Ctrl+K
for every action; the keys are described in
.Pa docs/query-screen.md .
Without a
.Ar connection ,
it reopens the last session.
A URL or file opened before there is any project goes into one named
.Dq default ,
so its tabs and history are kept like any other.
.Pp
With
.Fl e
it runs SQL without the user interface, prints the result to standard
output and messages to standard error, and exits with a code that says
what happened (see
.Sx EXIT STATUS ) .
It uses the same saved connections, keychain passwords, connection modes
and timeouts as the user interface (see
.Sx SAFETY ) .
.Pp
.Ar connection
is one of:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
a URL:
.Li postgres://user@host:port/db ,
.Li mysql://user@host/db
or
.Li sqlite:path ;
.It
a SQLite file (a path with a slash, or ending in
.Pa .db ,
.Pa .sqlite ,
.Pa .sqlite3
or
.Pa .db3 ) ,
or
.Li :memory: ;
.It
the name of a saved connection in the project (case-insensitive).
.El
.Pp
For
.Fl e ,
.Fl -import
and
.Fl -test ,
it defaults to
.Ev DATABASE_URL .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p , Fl -project Ar name
Use this project (default: the last one opened).
Its saved connections are the names
.Ar connection
can refer to.
.It Fl c , Fl -connection Ar connection
The same as the
.Ar connection
argument.
.It Fl e , Fl -execute Ar sql
Run
.Ar sql
without the user interface and print the result.
.Ar sql
may hold several statements.
.Sq -
reads it from standard input.
A script whose statements return no result set (no
.Li SELECT ,
no
.Li RETURNING )
prints
.Dq OK: ... row(s) affected
to standard error.
.It Fl f , Fl -format Ar format
The output of
.Fl e :
.Cm table
(aligned columns, the default on a terminal),
.Cm csv
(the default otherwise),
.Cm tsv
or
.Cm json
(an array of objects, one per row; values are strings or
.Li null ) .
TSV keeps each row on one line: tabs and line breaks inside values become
a space and
.Ql \en ,
and NULL an empty field.
For a script with several result sets,
.Cm table
and
.Cm json
print each one
.Po
.Cm json
as one array per set, one after another, which
.Xr jq 1
reads as a stream
.Pc ;
.Cm csv
and
.Cm tsv
print only the last, and say on standard error how many they left out.
.It Fl -limit Ar n
Print at most
.Ar n
rows of each result set.
A cut result says
.Dq (first N of M rows)
on standard error.
.It Fl y , Fl -yes
Allow
.Fl e
and
.Fl -import
to change data on a production connection, and allow destructive
statements.
.It Fl -create
Let
.Fl e
or
.Fl -import
create a SQLite file that doesn't exist yet (a missing one is usually a
typo).
.It Fl -test
Connect to
.Ar connection
step by step (DNS, TCP, login, TLS, latency) and show where it fails,
with hints for common errors.
.It Fl -import Ar file
Load a CSV or TSV file into a table; a name ending in
.Pa .gz
is read compressed.
The separator is found from the file (see
.Fl -delimiter ) ;
in a file separated by
.Ql \&;
a decimal comma
.Pq Ql 12,50
is read as a decimal point.
The first line must name the columns.
An empty field is NULL.
TSV files aren't quoted, and
.Ql \eN
in them is NULL too (as in IMDb's files and
.Ql COPY
output).
A missing table is created with guessed column types (integer, decimal,
boolean, date, timestamp, timestamp with time zone on PostgreSQL, or text);
an existing one is appended to.
On a terminal, a line shows how far it has got.
PostgreSQL loads the rows with
.Ql COPY .
It all happens in one transaction, so a bad line changes nothing (on
MySQL, a newly created table stays).
.It Fl -table Ar name
The table for
.Fl -import
(default: the file's name without its extensions).
.It Fl -delimiter Ar c
The separator for
.Fl -import :
one character, or
.Ql tab .
By default it's a tab for
.Pa .tsv
and
.Pa .tab
files, and otherwise whichever of
.Ql \&; ,
tab and
.Ql \&,
the first line has most of.
.It Fl -model Ar name
For
.Cm ask :
the model to ask, by its name in
.Pa config.toml
(default: the first one there).
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a summary of the options.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ask Oo Fl p Ar project Oc Oo Fl -model Ar name Oc Ar connection question
Has an AI model write one SQL query for the
.Ar question
(the words after the connection) over the saved
.Ar connection Ns 's
schema, and prints it on standard output, never running it; what the
model said about it, and how many tokens it took, go to standard error.
The model is shown only the tables that matter for the question
(see
.Cm gateway context ) ,
never rows, and its SQL is checked against the schema: a table or column
that isn't there goes back to the model, up to twice, and any still
unknown are named before you run it.
A model that isn't on this machine is shown a project's schema only if
the project shares it: the first time,
.Cm ask
asks (in a terminal) whether to send it this once or always for the
project.
Exit status: 0 with SQL, 1 if none came, 3 if the schema wasn't sent.
.It Cm memory Oo Fl p Ar project Oc Op Cm list | add | forget | confirm | learn | export
What the project's team has said about its data, shown to models when
they write SQL
.Pq see Cm ask :
facts such as
.Ql podelis memory add orders.status 3 = cancelled
or
.Ql add revenue is orders.total minus refunds
(a first word
.Ql is ,
.Ql are ,
.Ql means
or
.Ql equals
says how it relates; otherwise
.Ql means ) .
A fact is shown with the tables it's about (its subject's table) or when
it matches the question's words, and only once it's confirmed; facts you
add are.
.Cm list ,
the default, shows them all, or those matching
.Ar words ;
.Cm forget
removes one by its number;
.Cm confirm
makes a fact a model suggested count (when it had to assume what a
value means,
.Cm ask
keeps the assumption, not confirmed, and says so);
.Cm learn
reads a folder of SQL migration files (and its subfolders, in file-name
order): each table's history, when it was created and its columns added,
dropped or renamed (dated from file names such as
.Pa 20240301_add_refunds.sql ) ,
a
.Ql --
comment right above a statement as a note on it, and
.Ql COMMENT ON .
Learning a folder again replaces what was learned from it.
No model reads the files.
.Cm export
prints the facts as text, to read, keep or review.
.It Cm doctor Oo Fl p Ar project Oc Op Fl -connections
Check the local setup: the config files, the keychain,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
the terminal, the programs that open result rows (each format's) and the
crash log.
With
.Fl -connections ,
also test every saved connection of the project.
.It Cm mcp Oo Fl p Ar project Oc Op Fl -name Ar label
Serve the project's connections shared with AI agents (the
.Dq AI agents
setting of a connection) to an agent over MCP, on standard input and
output.
The agent is served by the gateway
.Pq see Cm gateway ,
which
.Cm mcp
starts if it isn't running; each agent's session ends when it goes.
Agents see only those connections, never their passwords.
Each call runs in a transaction of Podėlis's own, read-only on the server
unless a connection allows writes; agents can't send
.Ql SET ,
.Ql BEGIN
or
.Ql COMMIT ,
destructive statements are refused, and on production connections every
call is rolled back, so writes only run as a dry run.
Everything an agent runs is logged, with the agent's name and version,
the session and the folder it was started in, and shows in the agent
activity window.
.Fl -name
(or the
.Ev PODELIS_AGENT
environment variable) sets what to call the agent in it.
.It Cm gateway Op Cm status | start | stop | models
The gateway is the background process that holds what involves AI
models: the models and their keys, and later what they're shown, their
tools and memory.
Features that need it start it, and it stops by itself after
.Li gateway_idle
minutes with no client (30 by default; 0 keeps it running), so it never
has to be started by hand.
It listens only on a socket in the config folder that only its owner can
open.
Without an argument it runs in the foreground until
.Ic Ctrl+C ,
SIGTERM or the idle time;
.Cm status
says whether one runs (exit status 1 if not),
.Cm start
starts it in the background, and
.Cm stop
stops it.
.Cm models
lists the models set up in
.Pa config.toml
(the
.Li [[models]]
entries, the first being the default) and where each one's key comes
from, never the key.
.It Cm gateway key Ar model
Asks for the model's API key without showing it and stores it in the OS
keychain (service
.Li podelis ,
account
.Li model:\& Ns Ar model ) ,
where only the gateway reads it.
.It Cm gateway test Op Ar model
Asks the model (the default one without an argument) for one word and
prints the answer, the tokens and the time: a check that its address and
key work.
It costs a few tokens.
.It Cm gateway context Ar connection question
Shows what a model would be shown of the saved
.Ar connection Ns 's
database for the
.Ar question
(the words after the connection), and sends nothing to any model: the
tables that matter, each with why (its name, a column's name or the
database's comment matched the question's words, or a foreign key joins it
to such a table), then their columns, keys, where foreign keys point, and
the database's comments on them.
Never rows.
The connection is opened read-only, as
.Nm
opens it (the keychain or its password command).
.El
.Sh SAFETY
.Fl e
and
.Fl -import
follow the connection's mode:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It read-only
Statements that change data are refused before they are sent, and the
session is opened read-only on the server (PostgreSQL's
.Li default_transaction_read_only ,
MySQL's
.Li SET SESSION TRANSACTION READ ONLY ,
a read-only SQLite file).
.It production
Changing data needs
.Fl y .
.El
.Pp
Destructive statements need
.Fl y
on any connection:
.Li DROP ,
.Li TRUNCATE ,
.Li DELETE
or
.Li UPDATE
without
.Li WHERE ,
and an
.Li ALTER
that drops a column or a key.
.Pp
.Fl y
allows both, so a script that needs it for one harmless reason allows
.Li DROP TABLE
too.
.Pp
These rules catch mistakes; they aren't a security boundary.
.Nm
reads the SQL to decide what changes data, so a function that writes
looks like a read, and a script can switch its own session back to
read-write.
For unattended scripts, cron jobs and AI agents, connect as a database
user that has only the rights the job needs, and use a read-only
connection as a second layer.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev DATABASE_URL
The connection for
.Fl e ,
.Fl -import
and
.Fl -test
when none is given.
Use it (or a URL) on servers and in CI, where there may be no keychain
for saved connections' passwords.
Without a keychain, a saved connection still connects: with its password
command, PostgreSQL's
.Pa ~/.pgpass
or
.Ev PGPASSWORD ,
or MySQL's
.Ev MYSQL_PWD
or
.Pa ~/.my.cnf
.Pq its Li [client] No or Li [mysql] password: only for the host it names, or this machine if it names none .
.It Ev PODELIS_CONFIG_DIR
Use this folder instead of the
.Pa podelis
folder of the OS config directory.
Keep it short: the gateway's socket in it can be at most 103 characters long.
.It Ev ANTHROPIC_API_KEY , Ev OPENAI_API_KEY
The API key of a model in
.Pa config.toml
at Anthropic's or OpenAI's own address, when it has none in the keychain
.Pq Cm gateway key
and no
.Li key_command .
Only the gateway reads keys, from the environment it was started with:
after setting one, run
.Nm Cm gateway stop .
.It Ev PODELIS_MODEL
Set for a model's
.Li key_command :
the model's name in
.Pa config.toml .
.It Ev PODELIS_LOG
Write a debug log to
.Pa podelis.log ,
for example
.Li debug
or
.Li podelis=trace
(a tracing-subscriber filter).
The log includes the SQL run.
.It Ev PAGER
Opens result rows
.Pq Ic o No in the app
in the terminal when there's no desktop to show an app on: on macOS over
.Xr ssh 1 ,
on Linux without a display (an
.Xr ssh 1
session without X forwarding, a console).
.Li less -S
when it's unset.
Not used for a format
.Li [viewers]
in
.Pa config.toml
names a program for, or for TSV with the older
.Li viewer
setting; with a desktop, the system's app for the type opens the rows.
.It Ev COLORTERM
.Li truecolor
or
.Li 24bit
turns on 24-bit colours; otherwise the nearest of the 256 standard
colours is used.
.El
.Sh FILES
In the
.Pa podelis
folder of the OS config directory
.Pa ( ~/Library/Application Support/podelis
on macOS,
.Pa ~/.config/podelis
on Linux), or in
.Ev PODELIS_CONFIG_DIR .
Files are created readable only by their owner.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa config.toml
Settings (optional, never written by
.Nm ) .
.It Pa connections.toml
Projects and their connections.
Passwords are kept in the OS keychain under the service
.Li podelis ,
not here.
A connection's
.Li password_command ,
such as
.Li [\(dqpass\(dq, \(dqshow\(dq, \(dqdb/prod\(dq] ,
is a program that prints its password instead; it runs without a shell
and gets the connection in
.Ev PODELIS_HOST ,
.Ev PODELIS_PORT ,
.Ev PODELIS_USER ,
.Ev PODELIS_DATABASE
and
.Ev PODELIS_ENGINE .
.It Pa history/ Ns Ar project-id Ns Pa .jsonl
Each project's query history.
.It Pa drafts/ Ns Ar project-id Ns Pa .json
Each project's open tabs and session.
.It Pa queries/ Ns Ar project-id Ns Pa .json
Each project's saved queries.
.It Pa podelis.log
The debug log (only with
.Ev PODELIS_LOG ) .
.It Pa crash.log
What happened if
.Nm
crashed.
.It Pa opened/
Result rows opened
.Pq Ic o No in the app ,
one file each: those a day old are removed the next time rows are opened,
one shown in the terminal as soon as its viewer exits.
.Pa open.log
holds what the last app started said, if it failed.
.It Pa memory/ Ns Ar project-id Ns Pa .db
Each project's facts
.Pq Cm memory ,
in SQLite.
.It Pa gateway/
The gateway's socket, its lock file, and
.Pa calls.jsonl :
each call to it (when, which client, which method, the outcome), never
what was asked.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
For
.Fl e ,
.Fl -import
and
.Fl -test :
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
The SQL (or the import) failed on the server.
.It 2
Usage: a bad option, an unknown project or connection, a missing SQLite
file.
.It 3
Refused: a write on a read-only connection, or a production or
destructive change without
.Fl y .
.It 4
Couldn't connect: network, login, password command or SSH; for
.Fl -test ,
a step failed.
.It 5
The connection's query timeout ran out; the query was cancelled.
.El
.Pp
For
.Cm ask :
0 the SQL was printed, 1 none came (the model said why, or it failed), 2
usage, 3 the project's schema wasn't sent to the model (not allowed, and
no terminal to ask in).
.Pp
Retrying can help with 4 and 5.
It won't help with 1, 2 or 3.
A write whose connection broke (4) may or may not have happened, so check
before running it again.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print a few rows of a saved connection as a table:
.Dl $ podelis prod -e \(dqselect id, email from users limit 5\(dq
.Pp
Export to CSV from another project:
.Dl $ podelis -p shop prod -e \(dqselect * from orders\(dq -f csv > orders.csv
.Pp
Run SQL from standard input against a SQLite file:
.Dl $ echo \(dqselect count(*) from events\(dq | podelis app.db -e -
.Pp
Every result set of a script, as one JSON array:
.Dl $ podelis prod -e \(dqselect 1 a; select 2 b\(dq -f json | jq -s
.Pp
In CI, with the password from a secret:
.Dl $ DATABASE_URL=postgres://report:$PGPASSWORD@db/shop podelis -e \(dqselect 1\(dq
.Pp
Load a CSV file into a new table, creating the database:
.Dl $ podelis new.db --create --import users.csv
.Pp
Find out why a connection fails:
.Dl $ podelis --test prod
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr less 1 ,
.Xr mysql 1 ,
.Xr psql 1 ,
.Xr sqlite3 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1
.Pp
.Lk https://github.com/taislius/podelis
